Fix frontend auth to match real FastAPI backend contract (401 fix)

The real lighthouse-rdm backend's global AuthMiddleware requires an Azure
access token whose audience is the app's own API (plus oid + non-empty groups
claims). The frontend was only requesting OIDC scopes, so MSAL returned a
Microsoft Graph token that the backend rejected with 401 on every data call.
Per the user's instruction, only frontend files were changed (backend untouched).

Changes (frontend only):
- lib/auth.ts: acquire/login with the app's API scope (default
  api://<clientId>/access_as_user, overridable via /api/config apiScope);
  added getLoginRequest() so login consents to the API scope.
- app/api/config/route.ts: also return apiScope from AZURE_API_SCOPE.
- AuthProvider.tsx: pass apiScope into getMsalInstance.
- lib/api.ts: fix UserInfoResponse to the real shape; remove dead
  fetchUserRoles/UserRolesResponse; add deriveRoles().
- AuthGuard.tsx: get roles from /v1/user/info (real endpoint) instead of the
  non-existent /v1/user/roles; use getLoginRequest().
- app/(dashboard)/page.tsx: read username instead of removed user_name.
- DataTable.tsx: gate create/update/delete + EditDrawer persisted banner behind
  WRITE_API_ENABLED=false (backend is read-only); edits fall back to local-only.

Verified: tsc --noEmit passes; app boots; dev-bypass UI renders. Architect
review PASS after gating the EditDrawer persisted prop.

Deployment prerequisite (frontend cannot fix): the Azure App Registration must
expose the API scope (or set AZURE_API_SCOPE) and emit the groups claim, else
401/403 persists. End-to-end auth is only verifiable in the real environment.
